WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.600
The Arctic is warming up
to four times faster

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.840
than the rest of the world.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.400
Ice caps are melting and
sea ice is retreating,

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.360
changing the weather and
disrupting marine life.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.400
To protect these polar ecosystems,
we need to understand them.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.800
And no one knows the Arctic

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.520
as well as the indigenous
communities that live here.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.680
Greenland is the world's
largest island.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.600
It is one of the most extreme
environments on Earth

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.080
where polar night or
24 hour daylight

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.560
can last for months

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.040
and temperatures can
reach -50.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.680
Inuit have thrived here since
time immemorial.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.360
But the Arctic is changing,

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.760
and it's happening in mere decades.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.160
Indigenous peoples of the far north

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.320
have been adapting to
change for millennia.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.760
A new generation of Greenlanders

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.040
is combining science and
traditional knowledge

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.600
and in the process, reframing
perceptions of the Arctic.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.120
In Greenland, journalists and scientists
come for one thing,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:18.680
for climate change.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.240
How do you see that as a Greenlander?

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.480
There's a very powerful narrative
around the Arctic these days

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.840
and Arctic indigenous peoples,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.520
that we are the place
of climate change.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.360
But we've lived here on these lands
for thousands of years

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.640
and there's a lot more to us
than climate change.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.560
What indigenous scientists can do
is to bridge some of that gap.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.640
As a microbiologist I'm interested
in understanding

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.600
how being in nature, eating from
nature impacts us through

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.920
connecting us to the microbiology
of our environment.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.680
Microbial species outnumber the
stars in our galaxy,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.320
and they impact every living
thing on earth,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.600
including Arctic biomes.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.120
What we're looking at here
is the downstream effect

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.160
of nutrients like phosphate,
silicate and nitrogen.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.320
how they affect the fjord systems
out there

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.560
specifically the phytoplankton.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.000
So this is a land-terminating
glacier, feeding in via rivers.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.600
And so what's the difference between
a marine-terminating glacier

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.400
and a land-terminating one?

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.120
The marine-terminating glacier

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.560
creates an upwelling effect
on nutrients,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.280
and these nutrients spike the
biological production

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.880
in the fjords.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.520
Well, when they retreat back, we lose
part of the microscopic plankton

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.280
and they are in turn food
for higher organisms

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.040
like whales, for instance.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.720
It's all connected, right?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.280
In Kalaallisut, we have this incredibly
meaningful word, Sila.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.280
Sila means the weather,
it means consciousness,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.280
it means the spirit,
it means outside.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.720
We're not separate from nature.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.040
When our minds are well, when the
Sila inside of us is well,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.280
we can treat our environment well.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.800
Right now, my work is very much
focused on building

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.840
the Sila biology program.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.360
We're going to be outside a lot like,
that supports mental health

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.240
but it's also an excellent way
of learning biology.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.320
When we eat from nature, when what
we wear comes from nature.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.760
We are conscious that what we do
has consequences.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.440
People assume that it will have a
really strong smell,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.480
but it's not strong, it's just
very particular.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.480
It's very particular to iginneq.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.400
I'm currently so excited that I can't even
think, I'm just in eating mode.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.360
My biggest hope is that we'll have
Indigenous students

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.240
coming out of this program who can use
the skills that they've learned

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.320
to make a difference for
their communities.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.120
Arctic peoples are uniquely placed to
understand what is happening here

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.440
and what it means to heal our
relationship with nature.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:23.600
We just need to be willing to
listen and to learn.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.720
Prada group and IOC/UNESCO
launched SEA BEYOND

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.720
to promote ocean education
around the world.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.400
SEA BEYOND represents Prada Group's
long-term commitment

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.440
to supporting educational programs
that connect

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.920
the younger generation with
marine ecosystems.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.560
That's why Prada is donating
one percent of profits

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.280
from its Re-Nylon collection to generate
innovative approaches,

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.880
science-based solutions and
humanitarian projects

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.560
that address the global challenges
facing our ocean.

